WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Hello, everybody. My name is Ryan Johnson, and I'm a data science advisor here at Posit.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:16.000
And welcome to this month's Enterprise Community Meetup, where we'll discuss an end-to-end data science workflow using Posit Team.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:25.000
As a reminder, this will be a recurring event on the last Wednesday of every month, and we hope it'll serve as a good primer for those new to Posit Team,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
but also helps current Posit Team users become more familiar with our tools.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Every month, we will cover a different topic and highlight all three tools within Posit Team.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
We have Posit Workbench, Posit Connect, and Posit Package Manager.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:53.000
During and after each presentation, there will be a few members of the Posit Team in the chat to address any questions you have about our tools, workflows, licensing, and anything else you may be curious about.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:02.000
This month, we have a pretty exciting topic for everyone, especially those within the life sciences, healthcare, and pharmaceutical space.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:15.000
We are going to use Posit Team to create a Shiny application of clinical trial data in R, all while discussing best practices for working within what's known as a validated environment.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:26.000
As we go through today's session, I'll be sure to stop and note various ways Posit Team can help support a validated compute environment and encourage good coding practices.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Let's get started.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
I first want to make sure everyone on the call has a solid, high-level understanding of Posit Team.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Starting at the very top, we have our data scientists and analysts.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000
These are the folks writing code and creating insights using Posit Workbench.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:59.000
They can use whatever language they want, including R and Python, as well as any development environment they choose, including the RStudio IDE, JupyterLab, Jupyter Notebooks, and VS Code.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:11.000
For R developers, they may be creating insights like Shiny applications, pins, R Markdown documents, or APIs using the Plumber package.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:21.000
For Python developers, they have a home to create interactive applications using things like Streamlit, Dash, Bokeh, and Shiny.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.000
They can also create documents using Jupyter Notebooks or even Quarto, and APIs using Flask and FastAPI.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Once the developers create content, they need a way to share it with the people that need to see it, including decision makers, coworkers, clients, or maybe even just friends and family.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.000
That's the role of Posit Connect, which is our professional publishing platform.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:05.000
And finally, we have Posit Package Manager, which helps organize and centralize the great open source R and Python packages your team uses, as well as host and distribute any internally developed R and Python packages you've created.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:19.000
So for today's session, we're going to start within Posit Workbench, and we will leverage the RStudio IDE to create a Shiny application in R using clinical trial data.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:27.000
And then we'll publish this application to Posit Connect, all within the context of a validated environment.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Now, just for awareness, the clinical trial data that we'll use today is publicly available. It's been properly de-identified and cleansed for demonstration purposes.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.000
So before we actually dive into this workflow, let's take a few moments just to talk more about this clinical trial data set.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Now, what is it? Where does it come from?

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Now, as a high level overview, clinical trials, they are conducted usually to assess the efficacy of some disease intervention.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:14.000
So during the trial, data will be collected, organized, and eventually that data will find itself inside of an analysis ready data set called an atom or analysis data model data set.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:24.000
So these atom data sets are usually what's submitted to various regulatory agencies, such as the Food and Drug Administration or FDA.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.000
So here's actually a quick glimpse into what this example atom data set looks like.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Now, we are only going to focus on the columns that you see here, which include things like subject ID number, the treatment group, age, BMI, height, weight, and years of education.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:56.000
So these subject level variables are usually found within a specific type of atom data set called a subject level or ADSL data set.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:07.000
Now, while this data is not particularly large, it does contain numerous descriptive columns, and it can be a challenge to create static insights such as plots and tables to explore them all.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:15.000
So for this reason, we will create an interactive web application using the shiny R package, which I'm showing here.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:29.000
So this application will allow viewers to explore the data for themselves and compare various subject level variables between the three treatments groups, which we'll talk about a little bit more in a bit.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:37.000
So to start, let's open an RStudio session within Posit Workbench.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:46.000
So here we have in Posit Workbench on the right hand side, you can see some of the projects I've been working on, but we'll go ahead and click New Session.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:55.000
And you can see right off the bat that I have a choice between Jupyter Notebooks, JupyterLab, RStudio Pro, and VS Code.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So for today, we're going to select RStudio.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:06.000
And now before I start this session, I want to briefly describe our demo computing environment here at Posit.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.000
So we host Posit Workbench within a clustered environment, which is managed by a tool called Kubernetes, which you can see here.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:31.000
So for validated compute environments, it's often necessary that certain aspects of this RStudio session are controlled for, including what R version is being used, which R or system packages come pre-installed.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.000
And so that's where tools like Docker come into play.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:42.000
And here at the very bottom, you can see that I have a choice for various Docker images I can deploy within.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.000
And this can be customized to your team's needs.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.000
So for this demo, I'm just going to stick with the default image right here.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:55.000
And we'll go ahead and click Start Session.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:08.000
So while this is booting up, before we do any analyses, it's really important that we establish a new RStudio project for this clinical trial analysis.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
So this will provide us with a working directory for this project and only this project.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:21.000
So this helps ensure that the analyses we do here, that they won't impact anything else on our system or another project.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:28.000
And this is generally considered good practice for not just clinical trial data, but really all data science projects.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.000
So to create a project, we have a few different options.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.000
We can go to File at the top of my screen and select New Project.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.000
There's also a button right underneath the Edit button down here.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.000
Or if you go to the right hand side of your screen, you can see Project None.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.000
And this is actually a good way of knowing that I'm not currently within a project.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.000
So let's go ahead and create one.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:59.000
I'm going to click on this button and select New Project.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.000
So this is going to be a brand new project.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.000
So we're going to go ahead and create a new directory.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:10.000
But I do have a choice to create a project within an existing directory or pull in a project from Virgin Control.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.000
But we'll stick with New Directory.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Select New Project, the first option.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:24.000
And the directory name, I'll just say Test ADSL Analysis.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.000
I'll place this directory, this project, under my PositProjects directory.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:35.000
And then you'll see two boxes right here, Create Git Repository and UseRenv with this project.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.000
So I'm going to check those boxes and I'm going to hit Create Project.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.000
So while this is booting up, a quick note on those two boxes that we just checked.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:52.000
So to ensure reproducibility for your project, it's always a good idea to use Virgin Control

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.000
and to initialize the project with something known as Renv.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Now, once we finish our workflow today, we'll actually go back and discuss Renv in more detail

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
and how it can be used to record our project's package environment.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.000
All right, so here we are now within our new project.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.000
And you can see the name of it in the top right corner.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.000
So first step in our workflow is to upload that Atom ADSL dataset,

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.000
which can be found right here on the CDISC website or the GitHub page.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.000
So this is going to be the dataset right here, ADSL.xpt.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.000
So I've downloaded this file to my local machine.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:40.000
And to upload it to Posit Workbench, all we have to do is select this Upload button,

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:47.000
choose File, and I'll select ADSL.xpt, hit Open, and OK.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:53.000
And now we can see the file right here at the bottom in our files directory.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:58.000
So now that we have the data in our environment, we need to install some packages,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.000
some R packages that will be used for the creation of our Shiny application.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Now, in a validated environment, developers are often limited to a select number of packages

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
that their team has approved for clinical data analyses.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:22.000
So the question really becomes, how do I make sure I use packages from a validated repository?

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.000
Well, enter Posit Package Manager.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:31.000
So here in our demo environment of Posit Package Manager,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:37.000
you can see I have a few customized R repositories over here on the left-hand side of your screen,

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
including right down here a validated repository,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.000
which includes packages that we have classified as validated for clinical work.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.000
Now, this is just an example repository to demonstrate that it's possible to create

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.000
customized R repositories depending on your team's needs.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.000
All right, so we can start exploring these various R packages.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:08.000
But how do we actually install them from this repository into our RStudio session on Posit Workbench?

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.000
So to do this, we're going to select the Setup tab at the top of your screen.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.000
And I'm going to scroll down to the bottom right here.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.000
And we see using packages inside the RStudio IDE.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.000
And you have the choice. You can change the repository URL using the global options,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:32.000
or you can set it more programmatically by running the code right here at the very bottom.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.000
So I'm going to copy this code and I'm going to come back into Posit Workbench.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:42.000
And I'm going to paste it into my R profile script that you see right here.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.000
So I'm going to click on this.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.000
And I'm going to add a few new lines here.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.000
I write a comment to myself saying validated repo.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.000
And I'm going to paste that URL that I just copied,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:59.000
which you can see here is that validated repository on Posit Package Manager.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:05.000
So the .rprofile script will always be run as soon as you start a new R session.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.000
So I'm going to go ahead and save this file.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:13.000
And I'm going to restart R by clicking Session, Restart R.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.000
So if I close out of this .rprofile and come into my console,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:24.000
I can check my active repository by running options, repost, and hit enter.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:33.000
And you can see that I have my validated repository ready to be used and to install packages from.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.000
All right.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:41.000
So once we have all that, we can, well, for our Shiny application,

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.000
we actually need to install some of the packages first.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
So before I actually bring in the Shiny application, let's first install some packages.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
So I've actually copied these install script over here.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.000
And I'm just going to paste it into my console.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.000
And we have a few packages here that we're going to need.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:05.000
So we're going to install the Shiny package, obviously, to create our Shiny application.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:11.000
The Haven package is going to be used to read in this ADSL.xpt dataset.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:16.000
BSlib is used to customize the user interface for our Shiny application.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:24.000
And then ggplot2, scales, and plotly, these will all be used to create that box plot for our Shiny application.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:38.000
So I'll go ahead and I'm going to hit enter and install all these packages from that validated repository.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.000
All right. And there we go.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.000
So now the stage is set.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:47.000
So let's go ahead and create a Shiny application to explore this Atom dataset.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.000
So to do this, I'm first going to create a blank R script.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:54.000
So right here in the top left corner, I have this little dropdown menu.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.000
And I'm going to select R script.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.000
And I'm just going to paste the code for the Shiny application.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.000
We're not going to dive too much into the contents of it.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.000
But I do want to demonstrate its functionality.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
So before we do that, let's first save it.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.000
Hit this little floppy disk symbol.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:16.000
And we'll save it as app.r.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:23.000
And you can see right here, I can run this application within the RStudio IDE running on Posit Workbench.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.000
So I'll select run app.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:32.000
And I'll open up my viewer pane down here.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:39.000
And so this is the simple application that explores the subject level variables in this study.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.000
And it compares them between the various treatment arms.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.000
So here we have those that were given a placebo.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.000
In the middle, we have those that were given a low dose of a drug known as Xanamaline.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.000
And then another treatment group that was given a high dose of Xanamaline.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.000
And on the Y axis, we have the various subject level variables that we can explore.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Such as age, baseline BMI, baseline height, baseline weight, and we have years of education.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:20.000
So now that we've built the Shiny application within our validated environment,

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:27.000
it's now time to publish it to Posit Connect so that we can easily share it with the people that need to see it.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:34.000
So for instructions on how to publish a Shiny application from within the RStudio IDE,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:40.000
I'm actually going to enlist the help of one of our jumpstart examples on the Posit Connect homepage.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.000
So let me switch over to Posit Connect.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.000
And on our homepage right here, I can select publish and I can go to jumpstart examples.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:55.000
And you can see right here I have a portfolio dashboard Shiny application.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:00.000
So we're not actually going to use this specific app within this example,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.000
but we will use the instructions for how to publish to Posit Connect, which starts on step five.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.000
So we'll select get started using portfolio dashboard.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:16.000
And then I'll skip forward to step five, which is publish.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:24.000
So it tells us that we need to push the publish button, this little blue button, from within the RStudio IDE.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:30.000
And if publishing for the very first time, you'll actually need to grab the URL for your Posit Connect server.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.000
So I'll just copy that to have it on my clipboard.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.000
All right, so let's go ahead and do this.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:39.000
So we'll come back to Posit Workbench where I have my Shiny application running.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.000
I'll go ahead and stop it.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.000
And here's our Shiny application, this app.rscript.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:48.000
And right at the top, we see that blue publishing button.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.000
So I'll go ahead and click on this.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:53.000
And you can see I'm publishing this for the very first time.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.000
So I'll select next.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.000
And I'm going to publish to Posit Connect.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:05.000
And here's where you want to paste in the address for your Connect server, which you can see for us is actually auto populated.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
So I'll select next.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.000
And it's going to get this little pop-up menu right here to connect.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:11.000
And that's what I want to do.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.000
So I'll select connect.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.000
Successfully activated token.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.000
So I can close out of this and connect my account.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:24.000
And now you can see the Posit Connect instance over here in the top right quadrant.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.000
We can give our application, our Shiny application, a title.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.000
I'll just leave it as a default right here.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.000
And this is where you want to choose what's going to go into the deployment bundle.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:42.000
And so really we only need this ADSL data set as well as the code for the Shiny application, which is just showing over here.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.000
And then we'll hit publish.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.000
And so once we do this, we'll get this deploy tab that opens up.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:52.000
And RStudio pretty much takes care of this for us.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.000
So what it's doing right now, it's actually capturing my environment.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:58.000
So it's looking to see what R version am I using?

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.000
What packages am I using?

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.000
What versions of those packages?

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:10.000
And some other information about my environment and then replicates it on the Posit Connect server, which it just opened up to.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:18.000
And once that environment's been replicated, it then deploys my Shiny application, which we see right here.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.000
All right, so here's the running Shiny application.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:25.000
I can still interact with it just like it was running within Posit Workbench.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:28.000
And now, but it's hosted on Posit Connect.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:35.000
And again, the main purpose of Posit Connect is making it super easy to share this content with people that need to see it.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:41.000
So here within the access tab, so as a publisher, I have control over who has access.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.000
I can change the sharing settings.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Currently, it's set to specific users or groups.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:53.000
And I'm the only one, that's me, I'm the only one that can actually view or edit this application.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:59.000
And if I want to be very specific, I can maybe share this with Rachel.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:03.000
And now we would be the only two that can view this content.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.000
I have a few other options as well.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.000
I can select all users login required.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:15.000
And that just means that if someone has the credentials to log into the Posit Connect server, they can view this content.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.000
Or I can select anyone no login required.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:27.000
And that basically means that if someone has the URL at the top of my screen, which you can customize down here, they can then view this content.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.000
So it pretty much opens it up to the world.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:35.000
And the people that are seeing it, they don't have to know anything about Shiny, R, Connect.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.000
They can just view it like they would any other website.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:45.000
Okay, so to wrap things up, let's actually go back to Posit Workbench.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:57.000
And we're just going to take a moment to record our R environment so that anyone else or probably more likely our future self could reproduce our work.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:11.000
So some of the things we'll want to record include which R version did I use, which R packages we use and what versions of those packages, as well as which repositories we use to install the packages.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:18.000
Now, as I mentioned earlier, we're going to use an R package called R env to help out here.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:25.000
So R env, it's an open source R package, and its name is shorthand for reproducible environments.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:31.000
So we're going to use R env to record our R environment into what's known as an R env log file.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:37.000
As you can see right here, I'll go ahead and click on this.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:44.000
So this is the current state of our log file, which was created when we first initialize this RStudio project.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:51.000
And we can read it from top to bottom, you can see we're using R for this project version 4.2.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:21:02.000
And here are the active repositories when I first initialize this project, which will obviously need to update to reflect our validator repository.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:05.000
Now everything below this starting on line 11 right here.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:12.000
This is information about the packages in this project, including which version I need.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:16.000
Now, as you can see, we are only seeing one package here, the R env package.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:22.000
And so we'll need to update this log file to reflect the current state of our package environment.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.000
So to do this, I'm going to open up my console down here, clear my screen.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:35.000
And I'm going to run from the R env package, the snapshot function.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:37.000
We'll just ignore that message for right now.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.000
And we're going to see some text right here.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:51.000
So I think this is one of my favorite parts about the R env package is that it will actually inform us what will happen if we decide to proceed.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:22:06.000
So it's letting us know that all these packages that are not currently in our log file as represented by this asterisk will be written to the log file with the version reflecting reflected in my current R environment.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:11.000
So if this all looks good to you, which looks pretty good to me, do you want to proceed?

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:17.000
I'll hit Y for yes and hit enter.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:24.000
And so now if we open up a log file, you can see there's a lot more packages in here, but I didn't have to edit this myself at all.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:31.000
It is now reflecting my current R environment and all those packages inside and the correct versions.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:39.000
And so this file can be shared with other users or as a companion file for FDA submission, for example.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:45.000
It can be used to reproduce your R environment and your analysis results.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.000
